{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RTALepp1qmBRJYPMtuDuYd","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Structured Review: ProgressProbe\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThe paper addresses \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories — situations where agents produce superficially varied but collectively ineffective actions — and proposes a window-level progress estimation system. The core technical contribution is a temporal-aware label aggregation method that assigns position-dependent weights to labeling functions (LFs) based on empirically estimated leading/lagging offsets, followed by a lightweight GRU-based probe trained on the resulting weak labels. The system achieves competitive accuracy at dramatically lower cost than LLM-as-judge baselines, with demonstrated (but degraded) cross-agent transfer.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n**Practical problem significance.** Semantic stagnation in long-horizon agent tasks is a genuine and underaddressed failure mode. The distinction between structural repetition (caught by CDCS/CDSA) and semantic stagnation is well-motivated, and the window-level framing is more actionable than step-level PRMs in real deployment contexts.\n\n**Reasonable positioning.** The five related work categories cover the most obvious prior art. The core claim that existing weak supervision frameworks (Snorkel, FlyingSquid) assume i.i.d. data is accurate, and the application of data programming to sequential agent trajectories is a non-trivial adaptation.\n\n**Cost-efficiency result is the paper's strongest practical finding.** A 700× speedup and 850× cost reduction versus LLM CoT at only −2pp accuracy is genuinely compelling for deployment scenarios, and this alone gives the work applied value regardless of whether the temporal label model is theoretically novel.\n\n**Honest limitations section.** The paper does not oversell: outcome_bp leakage, cross-agent degradation, SE-only scope, and the limited scope of temporal signal (2/10 LF pairs) are all acknowledged.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1 (Major): The temporal label model's novelty is overstated.**\n\nThe proposed aggregation is position-dependent weighted voting: ŷᵢ = σ(Σⱼ wⱼ(tᵢ) λⱼ(wᵢ)), where weights vary with trajectory position. This is a well-known design pattern. Time-varying/position-dependent ensemble weights appear in the structured prediction literature (e.g., CRF-based label aggregation, HMM-based weak supervision) and in the sequential learning literature. The paper does not cite or distinguish itself from:\n\n- **Sequential weak supervision with structured dependencies**: Work on crowdsourcing and weak supervision for sequence labeling (e.g., Safranchik et al., 2020, \"Weakly Supervised Sequence Tagging from Noisy Rules,\" AAAI; Lison et al., 2021, \"skweak,\" ACL) directly addresses LF correlation and temporal structure in sequences. These are significant omissions.\n- **HMM-based label models**: Snorkel MeTaL (Ratner et al., 2019) and its extensions handle dependencies between LFs via factor graphs and HMMs. The paper claims FlyingSquid \"violates i.i.d.\" but does not engage with Snorkel MeTaL or the broader class of structured label models, which handle sequential dependencies more principally than position-dependent weighting.\n- The actual gain from the temporal LM is modest: +6pp vs. MV and +3pp vs. FlyingSquid, with the +6pp concentrated in only 2 LF pairs. If the temporal structure is exploited from only 2 of 10 LF pairs, the empirical basis for claiming temporal awareness as a contribution is narrow.\n\n**W2 (Major): Related work is severely incomplete — only 11 papers cited.**\n\nFor a venue targeting ML researchers, 11 citations is anomalously sparse and raises concerns about thoroughness. Critical omissions:\n\n- No citation of Safranchik et al. (2020) or Lison et al. (2021) on sequential weak supervision — the most directly competing prior work.\n- No engagement with the programmatic weak supervision survey literature (Zhang et al., 2022, \"Survey of Weak Supervision,\" arXiv:2202.05433).\n- Agent monitoring/progress tracking: No engagement with works on task progress estimation in embodied agents or game-playing agents (e.g., ALFRED benchmark monitoring, BabyAI progress signals).\n- Process reward models: The paper cites only Lightman et al. and Math-Shepherd, missing OmegaPRM, RLHF-based PRM work, and recent outcome-conditioned reward work that is highly relevant to \"outcome_backpropagation.\"\n- Trajectory anomaly detection beyond CDCS/TrajAD: Works on out-of-distribution detection and uncertainty estimation for agent actions are not discussed.\n- The \"LLM-as-judge\" category cites only Zheng et al. (2023), omitting Prometheus, MT-Bench variants, and recent calibration-aware judge work that could contextualize the −2pp accuracy gap.\n\n**W3 (Moderate): The \"violation of i.i.d.\" framing is partially overstated.**\n\nSection 3.3 claims that i.i.d. assumptions are violated and uses this to motivate the temporal label model. However:\n\n- FlyingSquid and Snorkel do model LF correlations via dependency graphs; the violation at issue is temporal ordering within a trajectory, not LF correlation per se. This distinction is not cleanly drawn.\n- The paper's finding that only 2/10 LF pairs show significant temporal separation (Table 2) actually weakens the i.i.d. violation argument — if 80% of LF pairs do not exhibit meaningful temporal dynamics, the i.i.d. assumption is violated in a narrow, pair-specific sense rather than structurally.\n- The paper should distinguish between: (a) temporal autocorrelation of labels within a window, (b) leading/lagging relationships between LFs, and (c) non-stationarity of LF reliability across trajectory position. These are different phenomena with different implications, and conflating them inflates the apparent gap versus prior work.\n\n**W4 (Moderate): Cross-agent transfer results undermine generalization claims.**\n\nA −9pp degradation in cross-agent transfer (vs. LF oracle −14pp) is presented as a relative positive, but the absolute degradation is substantial. Given that real deployment involves multiple agent implementations, and that the paper does not propose any adaptation or domain-shift mitigation strategy, the practical scope of the system is narrower than implied. The claim of \"systematic empirical study\" in Contribution 3 is partially warranted but the transfer results suggest the system is more environment-specific than claimed.\n\n**W5 (Minor): κ=0.68 is borderline for gold set validation.**\n\nCohen's κ=0.68 falls at the lower end of \"substantial agreement\" (Landis & Koch, 1977). For a system whose primary evaluation rests on a gold set, this borderline agreement warrants either additional annotator validation, inter-rater reliability analysis per label class, or acknowledgment that gold set uncertainty propagates into all downstream F1 figures. The paper acknowledges gold set quality as a limitation but does not quantify how κ=0.68 affects the confidence intervals on reported metrics.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1 (Novelty):** How does the temporal label model differ from Snorkel MeTaL's HMM-based structured label model or from sequence-aware weak supervision frameworks such as Safranchik et al. (2020)? These works also handle temporal/sequential structure in labeling functions. The paper should explicitly compare against these methods, ideally empirically.\n\n**Q2 (Scope of temporal signal):** Table 2 shows only 2/10 LF pairs with significant temporal separation. Does the temporal LM provide any benefit on trajectories where these 2 pairs are absent or uninformative? If not, is the temporal label model effectively a specialized heuristic for 2 specific LF interactions rather than a general contribution?\n\n**Q3 (Outcome backpropagation leakage):** outcome_bp uses trajectory-final outcomes to relabel earlier windows. Is the +2pp probe-over-oracle gap partly attributable to the probe learning to implicitly replicate outcome_bp's signal from context, rather than genuinely exceeding teacher performance? Has the authors evaluated probe performance with outcome_bp excluded from training labels?\n\n**Q4 (Statistical significance):** Are the +3pp gain (temporal LM vs. FlyingSquid) and +2pp gain (probe vs. oracle) statistically significant? Given the gold set size and κ=0.68 agreement, these margins may not exceed noise. Bootstrap confidence intervals or McNemar tests on the held-out gold set would substantiate these claims.\n\n**Q5 (i.i.d. framing):** The paper frames temporal dynamics as violating i.i.d. assumptions, but FlyingSquid already models LF dependencies. What specifically in the i.i.d. assumption is violated that FlyingSquid's dependency graph cannot capture? Is it temporal ordering (indexicality), non-stationarity, or autocorrelation? Clarifying this would sharpen the technical contribution.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n**M1 — Missing citations in related work:**\n- Safranchik et al. (2020), \"Weakly Supervised Sequence Tagging from Noisy Rules,\" AAAI 2020 — directly relevant.\n- Ratner et al. (2019), \"Training Complex Models with Multi-Task Weak Supervision,\" AAAI 2019 (Snorkel MeTaL) — directly relevant.\n- Lison et al. (2021), \"skweak: Weak Supervision Made Easy for NLP,\" ACL 2021 — relevant.\n\n**M2 — Framing of \"semantic stagnation\":** The term is introduced without a formal definition before use. A precise definition (e.g., in terms of task-graph non-advancement or reward stagnation) would strengthen the problem statement.\n\n**M3 — Probe architecture choice:** The paper uses a 2-layer GRU but does not ablate against simpler baselines (e.g., logistic regression on window embeddings, mean-pooled MLP). Given the frozen encoder, it is unclear whether the GRU's sequential modeling adds value over a simple classifier.\n\n**M4 — \"Student-exceeds-teacher\" claim:** The +2pp probe over LF oracle framing is standard in knowledge distillation literature but requires that the oracle is a valid ceiling. If outcome_bp introduces leakage, the oracle may be artificially deflated, making \"exceeding\" it uninformative.\n\n**M5 — Technical report citation (Qwen3-Embedding):** A 2025 technical report as a primary backbone citation is acceptable but should note model availability and reproducibility constraints for reviewers attempting replication.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 3 — Weak Reject**\n\n**Justification:** The paper addresses a real and practically significant problem, and the cost-efficiency result (700×/850× vs. LLM-as-judge at −2pp) is the most compelling contribution. However, the paper's core technical novelty claim — temporal-aware label aggregation as a solution to i.i.d. violations in sequential weak supervision — is not adequately differentiated from existing structured label models (Snorkel MeTaL, sequential weak supervision frameworks). The related work section is critically incomplete with only 11 citations, omitting the most directly comparable prior work. The empirical basis for temporal awareness is narrow (2/10 LF pairs), and the marginal gains over baselines (+3pp vs. FlyingSquid, +2pp probe vs. oracle) lack statistical significance testing. Cross-agent transfer degradation is substantial and unmitigated.\n\nThe work is not without merit and could reach acceptance after: (1) a thorough engagement with sequential weak supervision literature, (2) statistical significance testing of all margin claims, (3) clarification of novelty relative to structured label models, and (4) a more honest scope framing given transfer degradation. As submitted, the contribution claims exceed the evidence and the related work coverage is insufficient for a top ML venue.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Confidence\n\n**Confidence: 4 / 5**\n\nI am familiar with weak supervision frameworks, structured label models, and LLM agent evaluation literature. My lower uncertainty is around the very recent (2024–2025) agent-specific literature where I may not have complete coverage of all concurrent work. The core novelty and related work assessments are made with high confidence."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ab495e248c19325a5 (use SendMessage with to: 'ab495e248c19325a5' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 18450\ntool_uses: 0\nduration_ms: 66371</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c8e84f15-6e95-48e4-bcda-53ea5ffe2848","uuid":"df905757-ee94-44ff-a610-47fe33d20607","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:49:35.686Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review. Your focus areas are **novelty/originality** and **related work positioning**. Review the paper below with a critical but fair lens. Rate on a 6-point scale (1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Target venue acceptance rate ~25%.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"ProgressProbe: Temporal-Aware Weak Supervision for Window-Level Progress Estimation in LLM Agent Trajectories\"\n\n**Problem**: LLM agents exhibit \"semantic stagnation\" — superficially diverse but collectively ineffective action sequences during long-horizon tasks. Existing methods (cycle detectors, PRMs, self-reflection, LLM-as-judge) don't fully address window-level progress estimation.\n\n**Contributions claimed**:\n1. An empirical analysis of LF temporal dynamics on agent trajectories, revealing leading/lagging structure that violates i.i.d. assumptions (§3.3)\n2. A temporal-aware label aggregation method that exploits domain-specific leading/lagging indicators for improved weak supervision on sequential data (§3.4)\n3. A systematic empirical study including cross-implementation transfer, LLM-as-judge comparison, and extensive ablations (§4)\n\n**Related Work (§2)** covers 5 categories:\n- Agent Failure Detection: CDCS/CDSA [Chen et al., 2024], SpecRA [Wang et al., 2024a], TrajAD [Zhang et al., 2024] — structural repetition / anomaly detection, miss semantic stagnation\n- Process Reward Models: PRMs [Lightman et al., 2023; Wang et al., 2024b] — step-level scores, require expensive per-step labels, lose temporal dynamics\n- Agent Self-Reflection: Reflexion [Shinn et al., 2023], LATS [Zhou et al., 2023], Tree of Thoughts [Yao et al., 2023] — intervention mechanisms relying on the stagnating model itself\n- Data Programming & Weak Supervision: Snorkel [Ratner et al., 2017], FlyingSquid [Fu et al., 2020] — both assume i.i.d. data points\n- LLM-as-Judge: [Zheng et al., 2023] — high accuracy but impractical latency/cost\n\n**Method (3 layers)**:\n1. **5 Labeling Functions (LFs)**: command_repetition, diversity_drop, error_pattern, tool_misuse, outcome_backpropagation\n2. **Temporal Label Model**: Position-dependent weighted voting: ŷ_i = σ(Σ_j w_j(t_i) λ_j(w_i)), where weights depend on empirically estimated temporal offsets. Achieves +6pp vs MV, +3pp vs FlyingSquid on gold set.\n3. **ProgressProbe**: 2-layer GRU (542K params) on frozen Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B. 0.80 gold macro-F1 at 4ms.\n\n**Key quantitative results**:\n- Temporal LM vs MV: +6pp (but concentrated in 2 LF pairs; see Table 4 ablation)\n- Temporal LM vs FlyingSquid: +3pp (moderate, may not be statistically significant)\n- Probe vs LF Oracle: +2pp (weak student-exceeds-teacher)\n- Probe vs LLM CoT: -2pp accuracy, but 700× faster, 850× cheaper\n- Cross-agent transfer: -9pp degradation (vs LF oracle -14pp)\n- Only 2/10 LF pairs show significant temporal separation (Table 2)\n- κ=0.68 inter-annotator agreement (borderline)\n\n**Limitations acknowledged**: gold set quality, outcome_bp dependency/leakage, cross-agent degradation, SE-only scope, temporal LM generality limited to 2 LF pairs.\n\n## Total references: 11 papers cited\n\nReferences list:\n1. Chen, X., et al. (2024). CDCS and CDSA: Cycle detection for stagnation in LLM agent trajectories. Proc. ICSE.\n2. Fu, D. Y., et al. (2020). Fast and three-rious: Speeding up weak supervision with triplet methods. Proc. ICML.\n3. Lightman, H., et al. (2023). Let's verify step by step. arXiv:2305.20050.\n4. Ratner, A., et al. (2017). Snorkel: Rapid training data creation with weak supervision. Proc. VLDB.\n5. Shinn, N., et al. (2023). Reflexion. Proc. NeurIPS.\n6. Wang, Y. (2024a). SpecRA. arXiv preprint.\n7. Wang, P., et al. (2024b). Math-Shepherd. Proc. ACL.\n8. Yao, S., et al. (2023). Tree of Thoughts. Proc. NeurIPS.\n9. Zhang, L., et al. (2024). TrajAD. arXiv preprint.\n10. Zheng, L., et al. (2023). LLM-as-a-Judge. Proc. NeurIPS.\n11. Zhou, A., et al. (2023). LATS. arXiv:2310.04406.\n12. Qwen Team. (2025). Qwen3-Embedding. Technical report.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProvide a structured review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (novelty, positioning against prior work, significance of contributions)\n3. **Weaknesses** (limited novelty, missing related work, over-claiming, positioning issues)\n4. **Questions for Authors** (specific clarifications on novelty claims and positioning)\n5. **Minor Issues** (presentation, missing citations, framing)\n6. **Overall Assessment** (score with justification)\n7. **Confidence** (1-5 scale)\n\nBe thorough but fair. Focus on:\n- Whether the temporal label model is genuinely novel vs. a straightforward weighted voting scheme\n- Whether the \"violation of i.i.d.\" framing is well-justified or overstated\n- Completeness of related work (are major areas missing?)\n- Whether contribution claims match the evidence\n- Significance and potential impact of the work","agentId":"ab495e248c19325a5","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Structured Review: ProgressProbe\n\n---\n\n### 1. Summary\n\nThe paper addresses \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories — situations where agents produce superficially varied but collectively ineffective actions — and proposes a window-level progress estimation system. The core technical contribution is a temporal-aware label aggregation method that assigns position-dependent weights to labeling functions (LFs) based on empirically estimated leading/lagging offsets, followed by a lightweight GRU-based probe trained on the resulting weak labels. The system achieves competitive accuracy at dramatically lower cost than LLM-as-judge baselines, with demonstrated (but degraded) cross-agent transfer.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strengths\n\n**Practical problem significance.** Semantic stagnation in long-horizon agent tasks is a genuine and underaddressed failure mode. The distinction between structural repetition (caught by CDCS/CDSA) and semantic stagnation is well-motivated, and the window-level framing is more actionable than step-level PRMs in real deployment contexts.\n\n**Reasonable positioning.** The five related work categories cover the most obvious prior art. The core claim that existing weak supervision frameworks (Snorkel, FlyingSquid) assume i.i.d. data is accurate, and the application of data programming to sequential agent trajectories is a non-trivial adaptation.\n\n**Cost-efficiency result is the paper's strongest practical finding.** A 700× speedup and 850× cost reduction versus LLM CoT at only −2pp accuracy is genuinely compelling for deployment scenarios, and this alone gives the work applied value regardless of whether the temporal label model is theoretically novel.\n\n**Honest limitations section.** The paper does not oversell: outcome_bp leakage, cross-agent degradation, SE-only scope, and the limited scope of temporal signal (2/10 LF pairs) are all acknowledged.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Weaknesses\n\n**W1 (Major): The temporal label model's novelty is overstated.**\n\nThe proposed aggregation is position-dependent weighted voting: ŷᵢ = σ(Σⱼ wⱼ(tᵢ) λⱼ(wᵢ)), where weights vary with trajectory position. This is a well-known design pattern. Time-varying/position-dependent ensemble weights appear in the structured prediction literature (e.g., CRF-based label aggregation, HMM-based weak supervision) and in the sequential learning literature. The paper does not cite or distinguish itself from:\n\n- **Sequential weak supervision with structured dependencies**: Work on crowdsourcing and weak supervision for sequence labeling (e.g., Safranchik et al., 2020, \"Weakly Supervised Sequence Tagging from Noisy Rules,\" AAAI; Lison et al., 2021, \"skweak,\" ACL) directly addresses LF correlation and temporal structure in sequences. These are significant omissions.\n- **HMM-based label models**: Snorkel MeTaL (Ratner et al., 2019) and its extensions handle dependencies between LFs via factor graphs and HMMs. The paper claims FlyingSquid \"violates i.i.d.\" but does not engage with Snorkel MeTaL or the broader class of structured label models, which handle sequential dependencies more principally than position-dependent weighting.\n- The actual gain from the temporal LM is modest: +6pp vs. MV and +3pp vs. FlyingSquid, with the +6pp concentrated in only 2 LF pairs. If the temporal structure is exploited from only 2 of 10 LF pairs, the empirical basis for claiming temporal awareness as a contribution is narrow.\n\n**W2 (Major): Related work is severely incomplete — only 11 papers cited.**\n\nFor a venue targeting ML researchers, 11 citations is anomalously sparse and raises concerns about thoroughness. Critical omissions:\n\n- No citation of Safranchik et al. (2020) or Lison et al. (2021) on sequential weak supervision — the most directly competing prior work.\n- No engagement with the programmatic weak supervision survey literature (Zhang et al., 2022, \"Survey of Weak Supervision,\" arXiv:2202.05433).\n- Agent monitoring/progress tracking: No engagement with works on task progress estimation in embodied agents or game-playing agents (e.g., ALFRED benchmark monitoring, BabyAI progress signals).\n- Process reward models: The paper cites only Lightman et al. and Math-Shepherd, missing OmegaPRM, RLHF-based PRM work, and recent outcome-conditioned reward work that is highly relevant to \"outcome_backpropagation.\"\n- Trajectory anomaly detection beyond CDCS/TrajAD: Works on out-of-distribution detection and uncertainty estimation for agent actions are not discussed.\n- The \"LLM-as-judge\" category cites only Zheng et al. (2023), omitting Prometheus, MT-Bench variants, and recent calibration-aware judge work that could contextualize the −2pp accuracy gap.\n\n**W3 (Moderate): The \"violation of i.i.d.\" framing is partially overstated.**\n\nSection 3.3 claims that i.i.d. assumptions are violated and uses this to motivate the temporal label model. However:\n\n- FlyingSquid and Snorkel do model LF correlations via dependency graphs; the violation at issue is temporal ordering within a trajectory, not LF correlation per se. This distinction is not cleanly drawn.\n- The paper's finding that only 2/10 LF pairs show significant temporal separation (Table 2) actually weakens the i.i.d. violation argument — if 80% of LF pairs do not exhibit meaningful temporal dynamics, the i.i.d. assumption is violated in a narrow, pair-specific sense rather than structurally.\n- The paper should distinguish between: (a) temporal autocorrelation of labels within a window, (b) leading/lagging relationships between LFs, and (c) non-stationarity of LF reliability across trajectory position. These are different phenomena with different implications, and conflating them inflates the apparent gap versus prior work.\n\n**W4 (Moderate): Cross-agent transfer results undermine generalization claims.**\n\nA −9pp degradation in cross-agent transfer (vs. LF oracle −14pp) is presented as a relative positive, but the absolute degradation is substantial. Given that real deployment involves multiple agent implementations, and that the paper does not propose any adaptation or domain-shift mitigation strategy, the practical scope of the system is narrower than implied. The claim of \"systematic empirical study\" in Contribution 3 is partially warranted but the transfer results suggest the system is more environment-specific than claimed.\n\n**W5 (Minor): κ=0.68 is borderline for gold set validation.**\n\nCohen's κ=0.68 falls at the lower end of \"substantial agreement\" (Landis & Koch, 1977). For a system whose primary evaluation rests on a gold set, this borderline agreement warrants either additional annotator validation, inter-rater reliability analysis per label class, or acknowledgment that gold set uncertainty propagates into all downstream F1 figures. The paper acknowledges gold set quality as a limitation but does not quantify how κ=0.68 affects the confidence intervals on reported metrics.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1 (Novelty):** How does the temporal label model differ from Snorkel MeTaL's HMM-based structured label model or from sequence-aware weak supervision frameworks such as Safranchik et al. (2020)? These works also handle temporal/sequential structure in labeling functions. The paper should explicitly compare against these methods, ideally empirically.\n\n**Q2 (Scope of temporal signal):** Table 2 shows only 2/10 LF pairs with significant temporal separation. Does the temporal LM provide any benefit on trajectories where these 2 pairs are absent or uninformative? If not, is the temporal label model effectively a specialized heuristic for 2 specific LF interactions rather than a general contribution?\n\n**Q3 (Outcome backpropagation leakage):** outcome_bp uses trajectory-final outcomes to relabel earlier windows. Is the +2pp probe-over-oracle gap partly attributable to the probe learning to implicitly replicate outcome_bp's signal from context, rather than genuinely exceeding teacher performance? Has the authors evaluated probe performance with outcome_bp excluded from training labels?\n\n**Q4 (Statistical significance):** Are the +3pp gain (temporal LM vs. FlyingSquid) and +2pp gain (probe vs. oracle) statistically significant? Given the gold set size and κ=0.68 agreement, these margins may not exceed noise. Bootstrap confidence intervals or McNemar tests on the held-out gold set would substantiate these claims.\n\n**Q5 (i.i.d. framing):** The paper frames temporal dynamics as violating i.i.d. assumptions, but FlyingSquid already models LF dependencies. What specifically in the i.i.d. assumption is violated that FlyingSquid's dependency graph cannot capture? Is it temporal ordering (indexicality), non-stationarity, or autocorrelation? Clarifying this would sharpen the technical contribution.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Minor Issues\n\n**M1 — Missing citations in related work:**\n- Safranchik et al. (2020), \"Weakly Supervised Sequence Tagging from Noisy Rules,\" AAAI 2020 — directly relevant.\n- Ratner et al. (2019), \"Training Complex Models with Multi-Task Weak Supervision,\" AAAI 2019 (Snorkel MeTaL) — directly relevant.\n- Lison et al. (2021), \"skweak: Weak Supervision Made Easy for NLP,\" ACL 2021 — relevant.\n\n**M2 — Framing of \"semantic stagnation\":** The term is introduced without a formal definition before use. A precise definition (e.g., in terms of task-graph non-advancement or reward stagnation) would strengthen the problem statement.\n\n**M3 — Probe architecture choice:** The paper uses a 2-layer GRU but does not ablate against simpler baselines (e.g., logistic regression on window embeddings, mean-pooled MLP). Given the frozen encoder, it is unclear whether the GRU's sequential modeling adds value over a simple classifier.\n\n**M4 — \"Student-exceeds-teacher\" claim:** The +2pp probe over LF oracle framing is standard in knowledge distillation literature but requires that the oracle is a valid ceiling. If outcome_bp introduces leakage, the oracle may be artificially deflated, making \"exceeding\" it uninformative.\n\n**M5 — Technical report citation (Qwen3-Embedding):** A 2025 technical report as a primary backbone citation is acceptable but should note model availability and reproducibility constraints for reviewers attempting replication.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Overall Assessment\n\n**Score: 3 — Weak Reject**\n\n**Justification:** The paper addresses a real and practically significant problem, and the cost-efficiency result (700×/850× vs. LLM-as-judge at −2pp) is the most compelling contribution. However, the paper's core technical novelty claim — temporal-aware label aggregation as a solution to i.i.d. violations in sequential weak supervision — is not adequately differentiated from existing structured label models (Snorkel MeTaL, sequential weak supervision frameworks). The related work section is critically incomplete with only 11 citations, omitting the most directly comparable prior work. The empirical basis for temporal awareness is narrow (2/10 LF pairs), and the marginal gains over baselines (+3pp vs. FlyingSquid, +2pp probe vs. oracle) lack statistical significance testing. Cross-agent transfer degradation is substantial and unmitigated.\n\nThe work is not without merit and could reach acceptance after: (1) a thorough engagement with sequential weak supervision literature, (2) statistical significance testing of all margin claims, (3) clarification of novelty relative to structured label models, and (4) a more honest scope framing given transfer degradation. As submitted, the contribution claims exceed the evidence and the related work coverage is insufficient for a top ML venue.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Confidence\n\n**Confidence: 4 / 5**\n\nI am familiar with weak supervision frameworks, structured label models, and LLM agent evaluation literature. My lower uncertainty is around the very recent (2024–2025) agent-specific literature where I may not have complete coverage of all concurrent work. The core novelty and related work assessments are made with high confidence."}],"totalDurationMs":66371,"totalTokens":18450,"totalToolUseCount":0,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7384,"cache_read_input_tokens":8110,"output_tokens":2953,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7384},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":2953,"cache_read_input_tokens":8110,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7384,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7384,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}}}
