{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GcCJ6Gs9yK2a7v9k9y5qqB","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"- **Strong agreement on W1 (temporal LM novelty):** R2's citation of Safranchik et al., skweak, and Snorkel MeTaL directly maps to my W6 (thin architecture motivation). I hadn't named these specific references, but R2 makes the novelty gap concrete: position-dependent weighted voting over label sequences is well-trodden. The paper needs to articulate a precise technical delta over HMM-based sequential WS, not just assert it.\n\n- **Agreement on W4 (cross-agent generalization):** R2 and I converge independently on the −9pp cross-agent problem. R2 adds a useful framing I hadn't foregrounded: the absence of any adaptation or domain-shift mitigation strategy makes this a design gap, not just an evaluation gap. This strengthens my concern — the paper can't claim a general-purpose monitor without at least sketching a transfer protocol.\n\n- **Agreement on Q4 / my W3 (statistical significance):** R2's question about whether +3pp and +2pp are significant is exactly my CI concern. We're aligned: these margins are asserted without error bounds on a 400-window gold set. R2 frames it as a question; I'd state it as a flaw.\n\n- **Nuance on R2's W3 (i.i.d. framing):** R2 says the violation framing is \"partially overstated\" because FlyingSquid already handles LF correlations. I'd moderate this further — the paper's i.i.d. critique applies specifically to *temporal autocorrelation across timesteps*, which is distinct from *cross-LF correlation at a single timestep* that FlyingSquid addresses. The framing isn't entirely wrong, but the paper conflates these two regimes poorly enough that R2's critique lands. This is a presentation failure more than a conceptual one.\n\n- **New concern R2 raised — Q3 (outcome_bp leakage into probe):** R2's question about whether the +2pp probe-over-oracle gain partly reflects the probe implicitly learning to replicate outcome_bp is something I flagged structurally (my W2, circular dependency) but didn't push to this specific empirical implication. If the probe's training signal is contaminated by outcome_bp's own trajectory-end information, the \"probe exceeds oracle\" result may not be interpretable as genuine generalization. This deserves an ablation where outcome_bp is withheld from probe training entirely — its absence from the paper is a real gap R2 correctly surfaces."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a4e8aa07e056ab533 (use SendMessage with to: 'a4e8aa07e056ab533' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 15393\ntool_uses: 0\nduration_ms: 15460</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c8e84f15-6e95-48e4-bcda-53ea5ffe2848","uuid":"383c49a6-f5a6-4254-883a-26285b4f1e13","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:50:23.685Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 (method + experiments focus). You gave the paper \"ProgressProbe\" a score of 3 (Weak Reject). Below is Reviewer R2's independent review (novelty + related work focus). They also gave a score of 3 (Weak Reject).\n\nRead R2's review and provide your reactions: where do you agree, disagree, or have additional thoughts? This is a roundtable discussion step.\n\n## Your Key Positions (R1):\n- Temporal weighting gain concentrated in 2 LF pairs involving outcome_bp — framework generality unsupported (W1)\n- Outcome_bp creates near-circular dependency insufficiently addressed (W2)\n- Gold set too small (400 windows) for claimed precision of 1-2pp differences, no CIs reported (W3)\n- Cross-agent 9pp degradation undermines generalization claim (W4)\n- Claude CoT comparison methodologically uncontrolled (W5)\n- Architecture motivation thin (W6)\n- Overall: solid problem, impressive ablation coverage, honest limitations, but core novelty claim fragile and evaluation insufficient\n\n## R2's Review:\n\n### Strengths (R2):\n- Practical problem significance — semantic stagnation is 